  "Number": 29,
  "Question": "Five times \\(A\\)’s money added to \\(B\\)’s money is more than \\(\\$51.00.\\) Three times \\(A\\)’s money minus \\(B\\)’s money is \\(\\$21.00.\\) If \\(a\\) represents \\(A\\)’s money in dollars and \\(b\\) represents \\(B\\)’s money in dollars, then:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(a\\gt9,\\) \\(b\\gt6\\)",
    "B": "\\(a\\gt9,\\) \\(b\\lt6\\)",
    "C": "\\(a\\gt9,\\) \\(b=6\\)",
    "D": "\\(a\\gt9,\\) but we can put no bounds on \\(b\\)",
    "E": "\\(2a=3b\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The two statements translate to\n\\[\n5a+b>51,\n\\qquad 3a-b=21.\n\\]\nThe opposite signs of \\(b\\) suggest adding the relations to eliminate it. Since the second expression equals \\(21\\), this gives\n\\[\n(5a+b)+(3a-b)>51+21,\n\\]\nso \\(8a>72\\) and therefore \\(a>9\\). The equation then gives\n\\[\nb=3a-21>3\\cdot9-21=6.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\boxed{a>9\\text{ and }b>6}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
